\section{From a Definition to a Running System}

An evaluator on paper does not determine the size of a pointer, the
entry point of a compiled subroutine, or the form of a job on punched
cards. McCarthy's HOPL account of Lisp recalls how the IBM 704's 36-bit
word, with independently accessible 15-bit address and decrement fields,
made a two-pointer cell natural. The names \texttt{car} and \texttt{cdr}
came from accessing those fields. Stoyan's longer \textit{History of
LISP}, translated by Paul McJones and ChatGPT in the local edition, puts
that layout in context: early list-processing systems were already
making choices about how to obtain, link and reuse storage. The
selectors survived the specific packing that helped name them.

The 1962 \textit{LISP 1.5 Programmer's Manual} identifies Stephen B.
Russell and Daniel J. Edwards as programmers of the interpreter and
credits Timothy P. Hart and Michael I. Levin with the compiler and
assembler. The distinction matters. The interpreter could examine
lists representing functions; the compiler could translate known
operations into machine-language routines and use Lisp's assembler
to produce executable code. A system in which interpreted and compiled
functions call one another needs agreed conventions for representing
values and locating functions. It does not need every function to be
compiled before a user can run a test.

\section{What the Manuals Specify}

The \textit{Programmer's Manual} ranges from S-expression notation
and a universal Lisp function to property lists, arithmetic, tracing,
error handling, and preparation of an actual card deck. In the
interpreter, a symbol's property list could hold its interpreted
definition or an entry for a machine-language definition; distinct
properties identify definitions with different argument conventions.
Thus asking how a form was invoked is as important as asking whether
the function had been compiled. The manual's separation of the
language from its interpreter machinery is more informative than
treating every property indicator as a new kind of syntax.

In reviewing John R. Allen's \textit{Anatomy of Lisp}, S. H. Valentine
notes that the later book treats multiple language and implementation
choices, including dynamic versus static scoping. For instance,
searching a runtime association list for a free name and selecting a
lexical slot at compile time are not merely faster and slower versions
of the same operation: if the caller binds that name, the two can
select different values. A manual or test suite must say which binding
rule a particular implementation is meant to follow.

The \textit{LISP 1.5 Primer} gives the reader a route through the system,
including an \texttt{EVALQUOTE} supervisor and specific programming
conventions. Berkeley and Bobrow's collection \textit{The Programming
Language LISP: Its Operation and Applications} includes instruction
intended to bridge the gap between the elementary mathematics a new
programmer knew and the manual's terminology. By contrast McCarthy's
\textit{A Micro-Manual for Lisp---Not the Whole Truth} isolates
\texttt{EVAL} and its environment operations. He explicitly leaves out
such things as property-list operations, input/output and sequential
programs, and explains how a hand translation or a cross-compiler can
turn an evaluator into an interpreter on a machine that does not yet
have Lisp. The omission makes the evaluator intelligible; it is not
evidence that a historical installation lacked those facilities.

\section{Worked Test: Two Ways to Supply Arguments}

Lisp 1.5's top-level convention can be tested by giving \texttt{EVALQUOTE}
the function \texttt{CONS} and the argument list \verb|(A (B C))|.
It applies the function to the supplied arguments as data, so the result
is \verb|(A B C)|. To obtain the same result in an evaluator that accepts
one ordinary expression, the equivalent test is
\verb|(CONS (QUOTE A) (QUOTE (B C)))|. It is not
\verb|(CONS A (B C))|: the latter asks the evaluator to look up
\texttt{A} and to call \texttt{B}. This distinction is observable even
on a system with no compiler.

Now replace \texttt{CONS} in the test by a two-argument user function
with the same body and run it before and after compilation. The value
should be structurally the same in both runs. Run one nested case too:
have the new function return the result of another Lisp call so that
the test crosses the interpreter/compiler boundary. An argument
convention or linkage error can pass an isolated primitive test and
fail this nested case. A faithful reconstruction records which
supervisor and which function representation each test used.

The 704/7090-era details tell us what a particular system could execute;
they do not tell us what a different system ought to preserve.
Chapter~\ref{ch:ext-153} follows the effort to identify that common
contract when Lisp programs began crossing machine boundaries.
