\section{Reproducing Two Selectors on One Packed Word}

The address and decrement fields of the IBM 704 make it possible
to separate a storage fact from the Lisp operations built on it.
For a reproducible comparison, use a synthetic unsigned 36-bit
word with the address field in the least-significant 15 bits and
the decrement field at bit positions 18 through 32, counting
from zero at the least-significant end. Treat the remaining bits
as opaque. This model illustrates the extraction mechanism,
not the full historical cell encoding, instruction timing, or
allocator. Give the procedure a word and a new pair of field
values. It returns the old two selectors and a word with just
those fields replaced. Compare against a reference storing each
selector in an independent integer slot.

\begin{sudocode}
  \Input unsigned 36-bit word $w$, field values $a,d$
  \Output old selectors and updated word
  \State $m\gets 2^{15}-1$
  \If{$a\notin[0,m]$ or $d\notin[0,m]$}
    \State \Return range error \Comment{Do not truncate input}
  \EndIf
  \State $x\gets w\bmod 2^{15}$
  \State $y\gets \lfloor w/2^{18}\rfloor\bmod 2^{15}$
  \State $f\gets m+2^{18}m$
  \State $z\gets w\mathbin{\&}\operatorname{Not}_{36}(f)$
  \Comment{Keep nonfield bits}
  \State $z\gets z+a+2^{18}d$
  \State \Return $(x,y,z)$
\end{sudocode}

Take $w=2^{35}+2^{16}+6\cdot2^{18}+9$. The selectors
return address $9$ and decrement $6$, even with the two
outside bits set. Updating them to $12$ and $3$ yields
$z=2^{35}+2^{16}+3\cdot2^{18}+12$. Reading the new word
gives $(12,3)$, and the opaque bits at positions $16$ and
$35$ are still set. For a boundary test, update both fields
to $32767$, the largest 15-bit value. Both must read back
as $32767$; $32768$ must instead be rejected. Such tests
can be run with ordinary integer arithmetic, without a
machine emulator.

Extraction and reconstruction agree because the two masks
are disjoint and the inserted values fit wholly within
their masks. Adding into cleared fields cannot carry into
an opaque bit. Use an unsigned logical operation and a
36-bit complement; a host's wider signed representation
must not contaminate extraction. Matching this model explains
how the same two selectors can be implemented with packed
or unpacked storage. It says nothing about which field
values count as valid links in an actual Lisp heap.
